			<div class="content" id="post-content-4818618">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights</strong>: Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type</strong>: SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre</strong>: Horror/Mystery</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch</strong>: A lithostone, when used to repair books, causes content from those books to mysteriously, unpredictably manifest in the physical world. Accompanied by a universal memetic effect, with humanity accepting altered reality as fact.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative</strong>: Stone recovered by Foundation after previous holders are detained by police while trying to set their own bookstore ablaze. Recovery Logs review events leading to Foundation's acquisition through documentation recovered from the site.</p>
<p>Documentation records two individuals moving to small Irish town to establish bookstore/bindery. They visit the site of a previous bindery, a warehouse inexplicably abandoned years earlier, recover some tools and materials, but find no identifying information about the previous owners.</p>
<p>Later, news stories reveal ancient ruin discovery, believed to be Atlantis; overnight dissolution of anti-vax movement; successful AI invention by 26-year-old grad student. Binders suspect these unlikely events are linked to recently completed repair work, but can't test or control it.</p>
<p>Proceeding with business as usual, things go well until they don’t: a long-extinct volcano erupts causing thousands of deaths in rural Peru. Wracked with guilt, binders revisit the old warehouse, finding a journal and the ashes of business documents.</p>
<p>Shortly thereafter, news covers multiple child abduction and cannibalism ring busts and Greece’s rise to the world’s best in education, productivity, and happiness. Recognized as effects of work on book that rendered Atlantis and Swift's &quot;Modest Proposal&quot;.</p>
<p>Binders’ written record ends. Detained days later attempting to burn down their bookstore; routine surveillance of unusual happenings points Foundation to their location and the stone.</p>
<p>Foundation recovers warehouse journal. Research suggests earlier binders purchased lithostone from unnamed London antique store at a gross bargain and, after acquiring, used it as writing surface while brainstorming their new business, detailing the appearance and setting of their studio. Next journal entry relays finding “the perfect location” a few hundred miles away in northern Ireland.</p>
<p>Satellite imaging reveals said town appeared instantaneously in its current form and location at X time, prior to which existed an empty plain. Government acknowledges record discrepancies but maintains the town has been there for as long as they can remember.</p>
<p>(Potential running Foundation experiment log with different subject texts, a la SCP-500, 914.)</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber</strong>: &quot;So let it be written, so let it be done&quot;.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes</strong>:<br />
Gruesomely forwent full sentences, articles to meet 400-word max.<br />
Grateful for everyone's criticisms. Thank you!</p>

