			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825014">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Thanks&nbsp;for&nbsp;the&nbsp;Feedback</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide&nbsp;Reply</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Thanks, Dankaar,</p>
<p>I appreciate the feedback, first and foremost. The reverse approach, seeing the unaffected astronauts as the affected, is fascinating. Lots of potential for &quot;containment&quot; by keeping an eye on earth-bound events by comparing them to a non-earth-bound control.</p>
<p>In my drafts, neither of the original holders of the stone ever realize that the stone is the cause of the anomalous events. With all the tools that are used, the new location, the impracticality of testing individual books and tools, they're simply unable to pinpoint the source. Hence the frustration and the eventual unravelling of their characters.</p>
<p>Also, in my drafts (I'm writing this story out long form for NaNoWriMo), the SCP's effects are limited to the event itself (raising Atlantis from a repaired book on Greek Mythology), and public perception of the event. Centuries of evidence concluding that Atlantis was an allegory invented by Plato to make a point still exist unchanged. People can and occasionally do come across this evidence but, as the ultimate confirmation bias, they assume that it's all flawed or incorrect because they just <em>know</em> the place is real. In the long form, the protagonists realize that they <em>know</em> both realities are true before remembering that they'd set up a test to try to change a particular outcome. The SCP doesn't rewrite history, really, it just changes our perception of it, and so the physical changes are accepted as reality.</p>
<p>I too was struggling with how the Foundation gets in on this, but I really love the &quot;reverse effect&quot; perspective which alerts them to Foundation-level weirdness, after which they trace the effects back to stone&#8230; somehow. I was tempted to throw an MC&amp;D reference in with the antique store, perhaps the Foundation was tracking this artifact without knowing its effects, and the investigations merge.</p>
<p>Thank you again for the feedback and ideas!</p>
</div>
